-- The DATED-REGIME exhibit for the DMN exporter (spec §15, Knots 4 + 5).
--
-- This is what an OpenFisca parameter file looks like when it is re-expressed
-- as engine-evaluable DMN: a quantity that is not one number but a function of
-- the RULE DATE, and a downstream calculation that inherits that dependency.
--
-- Deliberately small and deliberately two-idiom, because those are exactly the
-- two shapes the recogniser admits (R10):
--
--   * `GST rate percent`               the PREDICATE idiom, as the Reg CF
--                                      corpus writes it: a one-parameter
--                                      DATE-typed helper applied to named
--                                      regime constants;
--   * `tourist refund minimum spend`   the INLINE idiom: the same comparison
--                                      with no helper predicate, written once
--                                      against a NAMED regime constant and once
--                                      against a bare `Date d m y` literal,
--                                      because those are the two things the
--                                      inline right-hand side may be and they
--                                      reach different code (`constantDay`'s
--                                      one hop, then the literal fold).
--
-- Both must lower to a SINGLE-COLUMN `hitPolicy="UNIQUE"` table over
-- `RULES_EFFECTIVE_DATE` with half-open interval cells, so the table is
-- gap/overlap-analysable by construction rather than by inspection. Both are
-- driven through Drools/KIE and Camunda 8 from `gst-rate.cases.json`, whose
-- boundary cases (the day of a change, and the day before) are what pin the
-- closed-low/open-high convention.
--
-- `GST payable on` is here to prove the chain end to end: a DATE drives a
-- NUMBER drives a NUMBER, across `informationRequirement` edges, in a real
-- engine.
--
-- Figures are the real Singapore GST rates (3% from commencement, 8% from
-- 2023-01-01, 9% from 2024-01-01) because a reader should be able to check
-- them; the module is still an exhibit, not a formalisation of the Act.
--
-- The heading below is load-bearing: `l4 export --to=dmn` with no
-- `--model-name` takes the model name from the outermost `§`.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

§ `GST rate`

-- Required by the rule-version axis: an unpinned `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` read
-- resolves to the localized current day, and localization needs a timezone.
TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

@ref Goods and Services Tax (Amendment) Act 2022 — second step of the Budget 2022 increase
GIVETH A DATE
`the 2024 rate change` MEANS Date 1 1 2024

@ref Goods and Services Tax (Amendment) Act 2022 — first step of the Budget 2022 increase
GIVETH A DATE
`the 2023 rate change` MEANS Date 1 1 2023

@ref Goods and Services Tax Act 1993, s 16 — commencement
GIVETH A DATE
`GST commenced` MEANS Date 1 4 1994

-- Before commencement there is no GST to answer for, so a rule date earlier
-- than 1994-04-01 is a curated refusal rather than an answer. Same device, and
-- the same reasoning, as the Reg CF corpus's ASSUME floor.
ASSUME `no GST rate exists before commencement on 1994-04-01` IS A NUMBER

GIVEN change IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the rules in force include` change MEANS
    `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` AT LEAST change

-- The PREDICATE idiom.
@ref Goods and Services Tax Act 1993, s 16(1) — rate of tax
GIVETH A NUMBER
`GST rate percent` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the rules in force include` `the 2024 rate change` THEN 9
           IF ^                            `the 2023 rate change` THEN 8
           IF ^                            `GST commenced`        THEN 3
           OTHERWISE `no GST rate exists before commencement on 1994-04-01`

-- The INLINE idiom: the same comparison with no helper predicate. Arm 1 names
-- a regime constant, arm 2 writes the day out as a literal -- both are legal
-- inline right-hand sides, and both must fold, because the inline form IS the
-- predicate form with the one-line helper elided and carries exactly the same
-- information. The annotation column shows the difference the source really
-- makes: arm 1 can be named (and cited), arm 2 has nothing but its day.
@ref Tourist Refund Scheme — minimum qualifying purchase
GIVETH A NUMBER
`tourist refund minimum spend` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` AT LEAST `the 2024 rate change` THEN 100
           IF `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` AT LEAST Date 1 4 1994          THEN 300
           OTHERWISE `no GST rate exists before commencement on 1994-04-01`

GIVEN `supply value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`GST payable on` `supply value` MEANS
    `supply value` TIMES `GST rate percent` DIVIDED BY 100
